{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml | Select-Object -Skip 550 -First 24; Get-Content docs/requirements/interfaces.toml | Select-Object -Skip 2057 -First 21; rg -n -F 'SN-029' docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml; rg -n 'verdict block|CRITIC|PLAN-CRITIC|log.md block|Reviewer:' project-trajectory/prompts project-trajectory/PROCESS.md\"","aggregated_output":"[requirement.SR-178]\r\ntitle = \"Text that has moved away from its acceptance record is reported\"\r\nsn_refs = [\"SN-029\"]\r\nboundary_refs = [\"B-02\"]\r\nhat_refs = [\"TEST-ENGINEER\"]\r\nrequirement = \"\"\"The kit shall report any recorded artifact whose text has moved away from the\r\ncopy recording its acceptance - stakeholder needs included - regardless of any\r\nStatus movement.\"\"\"\r\nrationale = \"\"\"THE DRIFT half, separable from SR-140 in the direction that matters: a record that exists and rides its approval commit, with nothing ever comparing live text against it, satisfies SR-140 completely and leaves every later amendment invisible. That is not a hypothetical - it is precisely the failure the git-history derivation had, and the reason a baseline on disk was owed at all.\r\nREGARDLESS OF STATUS MOVEMENT is the load-bearing clause, not a qualifier. The amendment machinery watches the Status flip, and the sanctioned staged-amendment path deliberately does not flip - so a rule keyed off the flip returns nothing on exactly the rows a sitting exists to judge. Reporting against the recorded copy is the only reading that survives that, because the copy is a baseline OUTSIDE the live file and a walk over the live file can never be one.\r\nTHE STAKEHOLDER-NEED RIDER is in the normative text rather than left to the acceptance because the need tier is the one a sitting most wants to hold and the one the amendment-watching rules were last to reach: each rule walks the tiers it is given, so without the clause the need tier is left off the walk and no rule reaches it. Needs are compared row by row under the same approved and traced split as every other tier.\r\nWHAT COUNTS AS MOVEMENT is one definition with one home, shared with the amend-without-flip guard: the id column and the Status cell are excluded STRUCTURALLY (the id is the join key rather than content, and folding Status in would make every flip read as an amendment and every amendment invisible behind its own flip), and the remainder splits into an approved half and a traced half. Two readers of one basis rather than two rules that agree until they do not.\"\"\"\r\nacceptance_criteria = \"\"\"An artifact whose normative text has moved since the copy that recorded its acceptance is reported whether or not its Status moved; an approved cell moving under a row still claiming approval is reported, a merely traced cell moving is not, and a row below approval has no claim to fall from and is never reported; the status marker itself is never read as the movement, so a flip is neither an amendment nor a mask for one; and stakeholder needs, assumptions and surrogates are held to the same rule.\"\"\"\r\npriority = \"S\"\r\nverification = \"Test\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nphase = 5\r\nda_refs = [\"DA-003\"]\r\naspect = \"process\"\r\n\r\n[requirement.SR-179]\r\ntitle = \"The acceptance record can only be written by copying live text\"\r\nsn_refs = [\"SN-029\"]\r\nboundary_refs = [\"B-02\"]\r\n[interface.IF-220]\r\nrationale = \"\"\"Re-accepting a risk is re-attesting its row, and a re-attestation moves no cell of the recorded copies, so which rows an approval act named must be written somewhere a reader may parse; the snapshot's stamp is prose that nothing parses. One typed entry per act, numbered, keeps two acts naming the same rows on the same day distinct, so the latest act always moves an accepted risk's anchor. The module that writes the record appends the entry in the same act and is the one that reads it back.\"\"\"\r\nowner = \"scripts/baseline_snapshot\"\r\nconsumers = [\"scripts/acceptance_record\"]\r\nchannel = \"file\"\r\ndata = \"the act ledger acts.toml beside the snapshot copies: [[act]] seq (a positive integer, unique and increasing in file order), date (an ISO day), approved = [row ids], reattested = [row ids], verdict (an optional non-empty in-tree verdict path)\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-006\"\r\nnotes = \"\"\"Appended by every snapshot act that copied a registry, the first signing included, and by none that copied nothing. A malformed ledger is refused whole, never read in part: the snapshot act refuses before it moves any byte of the record, trace.py reports it on the always-on integrity floor, and risk_acceptance_act returns no anchor with the reason. risk_acceptance_act reads the entries a commit added by comparing entry numbers with its parent's. The acceptance record's mirror rules skip the file by name, as they skip the prose stamp, since it copies no live registry. No party outside the kit reads the format: the owner writes it and reads it back, and the file reaches history as one more governed write of the act, committed with its copies. The verdict path is recorded for audit and no held-rung rule requires the act ledger's verdict cell.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-224]\r\nrationale = \"\"\"The owner's approval brief sets apart the owing chains the human-approval dial releases, and which rows the dial releases is a question the loop already answers for the rows it mints and the adjudication brief it composes. Reading that one predicate keeps the owner's surface, the mint and the adjudicator agreeing about who signs a row; a rung table in the brief would be a third answer to one question, free to disagree with the other two.\"\"\"\r\nowner = \"scripts/agent_common\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/trace\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"human_approves_spine(docs, registry) -> True when the dial holds the registry's rung or no rung map names it; False when the dial releases it\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nnotes = \"\"\"Cross-component crossing: trace is CMP-006 and agent_common is CMP-008. The import is deferred into the brief's rendering, so every other trace run loads nothing from the coordinator's primitives. The shipped dial holds every rung, so a repo that has released none sees the brief it saw before.\"\"\"\r\n267:[need.SN-029]\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\adjudicate-amendment.template.md:124:Write your verdict to {verdict}. One line per amended row, in the log.md block format:\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\adjudicate-first-approval.template.md:95:Write your verdict to {verdict}. One line per row, in the log.md block format:\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\adjudicate-disposition.template.md:46:Write your verdict to {verdict} in the log.md block format: one `- [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <what> -> why -> the concrete change -> @owner` line per finding. Then exactly one machine line:\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\critique.template.md:34:In coordinator use, Write your verdict to {verdict} in the log.md block format;\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\README.md:14:| `dual-plan-critic.template.md` | the critic hat | yes (`DUALPLAN-CRITIC`) |\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:68:Their specs are the Done-when you map coverage against; nothing here narrows the reading scope below, which stays the diff itself. Review ONLY (1) the diff of the work under review — the exact reading scope is `git diff {trunk}...{head} -- . ':(exclude)docs/iteration' ':(exclude)docs/reviews' ':(exclude)docs/log.md' ':(exclude)docs/log.d' ':(exclude)PROJECT_STATE.html' ':(exclude)docs/open-items.html' ':(exclude)docs/stage'` (three dots against the CURRENT trunk, so a station refresh never re-feeds you trunk's own merged work; session telemetry, verdict records and generated artifacts are excluded because they are records of the process, not the work) — and (2) the requirement surface it must satisfy: AGENTS.md, {process_doc}, the docs/requirements registries, and each work item's own spec file under docs/work/ (plus its `specref` target where that points elsewhere) — the spec of record. Grep the registry rows the diff cites; never read a whole registry file or docs/log.md end to end. If this diff adds or changes requirement rows (SN/SR/TC under docs/requirements), also sweep them against the EXISTING registries — the new rows AND the historical rows they touch — for any contradiction, overlap, or attribute/limit conflict, and raise each as a finding (mark it 'for clarity' at MINOR when it is a wording ambiguity sharper SN/SR/TC language would resolve, not a defect). If the diff under review is a DevStg-Reqs/DevStg-Tests approval (a Status-change commit closing a `[phase]-[g*]` gate), the batch-scoped approval hierarchy is a REQUIRED input: generate it with `{scripts}/trace.py --approve <phase>` and confirm the approved SN->SR->LLR/TC batch — its Requirement/AC, LLR Detail, TC Method/Expected, and any cited rubric — is coherent and complete before endorsing the gate. Flag status.md prose that contradicts a declared policy file's current value as a finding. Do NOT read or trust the implementer's own session notes or self-assessment — a leaked self-assessment collapses review finding-rates several-fold. Run the harness yourself and quote real output; believe nothing you did not observe — but run each instrument ONCE and quote its SUMMARY (`python {scripts}/check.py --jobs 0`: the Check summary block; `python {scripts}/trace.py --strict-integrity`: the final line), never the full advisory stream, which spends hundreds of WARN lines of your context on no finding. Drive the diff's REAL shipped code paths — construct the scenario and run the actual function or flow it changes; primitive probes and plausibility reading are supporting evidence, never the verdict's basis. Before hunting, name the worst failure classes THIS change admits (silent wrong content, fail-open, data loss) and hunt those first, severity-ordered. Hunt within the scope bound {process_doc} §6 \"Review threat model\" sets. Tie severity to reachability: a BLOCKER or MAJOR states a scenario reachable in normal use, and a fail-open, data-loss or security path counts only when a supported workflow or the kit's own routes reach it; a rare or contrived path is MINOR at most, or dismissed under {process_doc} §6 \"Review threat model\". An input outside the spec's declared set is yours to classify, not the set author's: one the change fails closed on is a coverage-extension note, written as prose outside the finding lines with no severity tag, so it never moves the count; one the change fails open on stays a finding at its severity. An APPROVE must mean you tried to break it and failed: map each spec Done-when item to its covering test or call it UNCOVERED, and where the diff adds a regression test for a fixed defect, confirm that test fails on the pre-fix behavior. For each test the diff adds or changes, ask: does it drive the interface the requirement names, not handler source? would it fail on a plausible wrong implementation? is each Done-when item covered? Of each new file and function, ask whether it repeats, exactly or in intent, something that already exists ({process_doc} §3, \"Consolidate, don't duplicate\"), and raise each repeat as a finding. This is an INDEPENDENT parallel review — do not debate another reviewer. Write your verdict to {verdict} in the log.md block format: one `- [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <file:line> -> issue -> the concrete change -> @owner` line per finding. When that concrete change ADDS a check, guard, warn, or invariant, the same line must also carry one clause naming why the defect cannot be made UNREPRESENTABLE instead — a stricter type, a deleted code path, or a single owning boundary that validates once and is trusted thereafter (the `antidote` skill's question, \"the smallest change that makes this fix unnecessary\", which you cite, not restate). Warn-first: this binds the remedy's WORDING, not the verdict — no finding is refused, downgraded, or blocked for want of the clause. It is NOT owed by a MINOR `for clarity` wording finding, nor by validation at a genuine trust boundary (where one lies: {process_doc} §3 \"When a guard is owed\"); only by a guard that compensates for a REACHABLE bad state the design could have made unreachable. Naming why construction is unavailable is enough — specifying the structural fix is not your job when it exceeds the diff's scope. Then exactly one machine line:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
